# Target C — audited §169 explicit direction gap from the §168 atomic gain

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only Type-II consequence; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The historical quantitative direction-gap argument combines audited §146 endpoint
stability with a distributional atom-versus-detour estimate for the occurrence-counted
outer direction measure. Section 168 strengthens the all-regime atomic train floor to

\[
A\ge2s+g,
\qquad
s:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad g:=0.0004.
\]

Feeding this gain into that argument yields

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}
\ge C_{146}+0.00089
=1.8204035379794445\ldots,
}
\tag{169.1}
\]

where

\[
C_{146}={4\over9}(\pi+s).
\]

The first zero of the resulting one-variable scalar margin is near
`0.0009024977029`; the certified endpoint `0.00089` retains strict normalized margin.

## 169.1 Endpoint-stability data

Assume for contradiction along a Type-II survivor subsequence that

\[
t:={q-k\over D}
\le C_{146}+\eta+o(1),
\qquad
\eta=0.00089.
\]

The audited §146 endpoint-stability inequalities give

\[
0\le\kappa:=\lim{k\over D}\le9\eta,
\tag{169.2}
\]

and normalized outer-walk detour

\[
\lambda_{out}:=\limsup {b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}
\le {9\over4}\eta.
\tag{169.3}
\]

Let `x=lim T/D`, and split train mass in absolute `D` normalization into atomic and
nonatomic parts

\[
A+N=x.
\]

The exact stopped-train and outer-walk ledgers give

\[
x\le5t-2\pi,
\tag{169.4}
\]

and audited §142 gives

\[
N\ge {t+\kappa\over2}.
\tag{169.5}
\]

Hence the atomic upper bound remains

\[
A\le2s+{9\over2}\eta=:A_\eta.
\tag{169.6}
\]

The new §168 lower bound gives

\[
A\ge2s+g.
\tag{169.7}
\]

Using (169.4) and (169.7), the nonatomic upper bound improves to

\[
\boxed{
N\le {C_{146}\over2}+5\eta-g.
}
\tag{169.8}
\]

This `-g` term is the only new favorable input; the atomic upper bound in (169.6) is
left unchanged to avoid double-counting.

## 169.2 Dominant-orbit leakage

Weighted Mantel on exact projective `pi/3` atomic orbits gives one dominant orbit whose
complement atomic mass is at most

\[
\boxed{
R_\eta
=A_\eta-{4s^2\over A_\eta}.
}
\tag{169.9}
\]

The function `A-4s^2/A` is increasing for positive `A`, so using the upper endpoint
`A_eta` is the correct worst case even after adding the new lower bound (169.7).

At `eta=0.00089`,

\[
A_\eta=1.9086306137279148\ldots,
\]

\[
\boxed{R_\eta=0.00800159605589257\ldots .}
\tag{169.10}
\]

## 169.3 The forced outer atom gains exactly `2g/3`

The occurrence-counted outer walk has normalized mass at least `pi-o(1)`.

- nonatomic trains contribute at most `2N` outer terminal occurrences;
- atomic trains outside the dominant orbit contribute at most `2R_eta`;
- the dominant orbit contains at most three projective atoms.

Therefore one dominant-orbit outer-direction atom has normalized mass at least

\[
\ell_\eta
={\pi-2N_{max}-2R_\eta\over3},
\]

where

\[
N_{max}={C_{146}\over2}+5\eta-g.
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
\ell_\eta
={\pi-C_{146}-10\eta+2g-2R_\eta\over3}.
}
\tag{169.11}
\]

Relative to the older quantitative direction-gap formula, the explicit atomic gain
contributes exactly `2g/3` before the nonlinear width-excess conversion.

At `eta=0.00089`,

\[
\boxed{
\ell_\eta=0.4326586411661879\ldots .
}
\tag{169.12}
\]

## 169.4 Atom-versus-detour lemma

Let `nu` be a weak limit of the occurrence-counted outer projective direction measures,
normalized by `D`, and put

\[
F=|\cos|*\nu,
\qquad
g_{out}=F-2.
\]

Projection variation of the actual closed outer walk and Type-II width convergence give
`g_out>=0`. Also

\[
\int_0^\pi g_{out}
=2\lambda_{out}.
\tag{169.13}
\]

On the projective period-`pi` circle,

\[
|\cos|''+|\cos|=2\delta_{\pi/2}
\]

as distributions. After translating `nu` by `pi/2`,

\[
g_{out}''+g_{out}=2(\mu-d\theta).
\tag{169.14}
\]

Suppose `mu` has an atom of mass at least `ell`. Put `r=ell/2` and use

\[
\psi(\theta)=
\cos^2\!\left({\pi\theta\over2r}\right)
\quad (|\theta|\le r),
\]

with `psi=0` outside `[-r,r]`. In the present application `ell<pi`, so the support does
not wrap around the projective circle. The function is `C^1`, piecewise `C^2`, and its
first derivative vanishes at both support endpoints, so distributional integration by
parts creates no boundary delta. Moreover

\[
\psi(0)=1,
\qquad
\int\psi=r,
\qquad
\|\psi''+\psi\|_\infty
\le {\pi^2\over2r^2}+1.
\]

Pairing (169.14) with `psi` and using `g_out>=0` gives

\[
\boxed{
\int g_{out}
\ge {\ell^3\over2\pi^2+\ell^2}.
}
\tag{169.15}
\]

Thus diffuse outer mass cannot cancel the cusp produced by an atom for free.

## 169.5 Strict endpoint contradiction

Using (169.12) in (169.15),

\[
\boxed{
{\ell_\eta^3\over2\pi^2+\ell_\eta^2}
=0.00406450115885619\ldots .
}
\tag{169.16}
\]

On the other hand, (169.3) and (169.13) give

\[
\int g_{out}
\le {9\over2}\eta
=0.004005.
\tag{169.17}
\]

The strict contradiction margin is

\[
\boxed{
0.00406450115885619-0.004005
=5.9501158856\times10^{-5}>0.
}
\tag{169.18}
\]

Therefore the hypothesis `t<=C146+0.00089+o(1)` is impossible, proving (169.1).

## 169.6 Scalar envelope and scope

With `g=0.0004` fixed, define

\[
A_\eta=2s+{9\over2}\eta,
\qquad
R_\eta=A_\eta-{4s^2\over A_\eta},
\]

\[
\ell_\eta={\pi-C_{146}-10\eta+2g-2R_\eta\over3}.
\]

The scalar margin

\[
H(\eta)
={\ell_\eta^3\over2\pi^2+\ell_\eta^2}
-{9\over2}\eta
\]

has its first zero near

\[
\eta_{env}=0.0009024977029\ldots .
\]

No theorem endpoint at that root is asserted.

The proof uses the explicit §168 atomic gap exactly once, only through the improved
nonatomic upper bound. Occurrence multiplicity is retained; no carrier-decimal theorem
or Type-I exchange is used. Contact-isolated source points remain outside all train and
rigidity kernels.

**Independent audit disposition:** PASS at audited-working proof-only scope. This is an
all-Type-II endpoint separation, not C-II closure. Type-I inactive containers remain
open.
